{
 "question": "Does a stream written from a recipe decode to the samples it was written from, in this decoder and in decoders that share nothing with it, and what do such streams show that real ones cannot?",
 "method": "crates/oadec-synth writes a TrueHD or MLP stream from a layout, a plan that says what codes each block, and the samples; it shares no code with the decoder, only the CRC and parity primitives. tools/synth_accept.py writes every recipe (`oadec-synth all`), runs FFmpeg (`-f truehd` or `-f mlp`, to 24-bit PCM), `truehdd` (`decode --strict --presentation 0 --format pcm`) and Dolby's decoder (the `dlbtruehddec` element of the Dolby Reference Player, `presentation=2`, on the stream wrapped in MP4 with `ffmpeg -c:a copy`; TrueHD syntax only) on each, each as a program on a file, and compares what it writes with the samples of the recipe byte for byte. It runs this decoder the same way (`decode -p 0 --format pcm`, `verify --json`). A recipe is confirmed when two of the three outside decoders give its samples. The restart blocks of real streams were counted with the segment parser of the decoder, a callback seeing every block with the state in force after its header, over the 41 TrueHD streams and five clips of the work directory. Eleven mutations of the decoding arithmetic (tools/mutants.py) were run against the suite of the commit this branch starts from and against the suite with the recipes in it. A few streams outside the recipes were written with the same writer to find where each refusal begins.",
