 "reading": "The writer and the decoder agree, and that would be worth little by itself: one hand wrote both. What makes the recipes references is that FFmpeg, truehdd and Dolby's own decoder, run as programs, give the samples of 23 of the 26 wherever they are asked, and that each of the other three has two of them behind it. The three exceptions are each a fact about one decoder. truehdd holds a segment to three blocks where Dolby's description allows four and Dolby's decoder and FFmpeg read five. The parser in front of Dolby's decoder loses access units of ten and of twenty bytes, which no real stream here has: the smallest real unit is 26 bytes, in streams of four substreams. And FFmpeg keeps its filter histories across a restart header. That last one is the finding. A restart header is an entry point, so a decoder that starts there has empty histories; whether a decoder that was already running clears them is something every encoder makes moot by coding the first eight samples without a filter, 282,022 times of 282,022. The written stream asks the question, two decoders that share nothing answer it the same way, Dolby's among them, and this decoder is on their side. Until that stream existed the suite could not tell: of the 11 mutations of the arithmetic, 3 passed it, a decoder that keeps the histories among them, and 2 more were caught only by a unit test that builds the bits by the decoder's own reading. With the recipes every one is caught by the decode of a written stream to samples that were known first, with no other test needed. One of the three that used to pass shows what the check inside the format is worth by itself: the wrong sign under book 3 moves an even number of samples by one, the eight-bit check word cancels, and only the comparison with the samples sees it. What this does not cover is stated in the document: matrices, more than one substream, output shifts and error protection are not written yet, and a recipe on which only one outside decoder could be asked would not be recorded at all."
